WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:06.640
The Golden Bowl's parking lot

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.020
didn't always look this great.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.300
Just ask restaurant
consultant Marvin Wells.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.400
The parking lot itself

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.050
was just a black top with faded stripes,

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:17.070
a wrought iron fence that
ran along the roadway.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:21.400
but then a clear view of the trash bins

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.560
and the channel, which sits behind us.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.070
None of it very appealing.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.930
None of it appetizing at all.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.230
Today it's got AstroTurf,

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:30.690
tiered levels, heat lamps,

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.710
shade covers, and a gardener.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:34.680
It feels as relaxing as its surroundings

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.060
in Santa Monica Canyon.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.790
It is actually the biggest
part of the restaurant

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:40.200
that we've had ever

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.250
we're busier than we've ever been.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:44.760
But this is not a local news story

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:45.980
about a neighborhood steakhouse

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.470
repurposing it's parking lot.

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:49.670
It's a story about a city
that loosened its rules

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:50.690
during the pandemic

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:52.520
and unlocked a new world of possibilities

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.910
for how the entire city
could look and function.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:56.560
And it all ties back to parking.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:57.790
I think the government can

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:59.380
comfortably step back

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:00.760
from trying to dictate

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.660
how much parking every single
property owner provides

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.000
on their property,

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.880
and that in stepping back,

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.690
they will make cities more
efficient, more equitable,

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:12.330
and more environmentally sustainable.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:13.770
Car ownership didn't start to become

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.840
the defining feature
of life in Los Angeles

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:16.977
until the 1920s.

00:01:16.977 --> 00:01:18.470
The Los Angeles automobile ownership

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:19.610
actually started to explode

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.410
because this was actually a great place

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:22.310
to own a car

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:24.570
because of the mild weather and so forth.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:25.403
Michael Manville is a

00:01:25.403 --> 00:01:27.410
Professor of Urban Planning at UCLA

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:28.660
where he studies parking.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:29.890
Well, what happened here is,

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:31.510
is what happened in a lot of places

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:33.050
when auto ownership started to grow,

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:34.820
which is that people would buy them,

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:38.330
but they didn't actually
have houses with garages

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:39.780
or driveways or off street parking.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.530
And so most people would
park on the street.

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:45.631
And so the concern was that
as more people got cars

00:01:45.631 --> 00:01:49.670
and as more people moved
to neighborhoods with cars,

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:51.460
the street parking would get congested.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.030
And so the policy response
that seemed easiest

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:55.250
to solve that problem

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.540
was to pass a law saying that

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.800
whenever you built anything new,

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.670
you had to supply off street parking.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:02.690
The city first imposed

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:04.767
minimum parking requirements in the 1930s.

00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:07.270
By the 50's, they were
part of the zoning code,

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.400
not just of Los Angeles, but
of every major American city.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.720
What they've done in Los Angeles,

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.120
because so much of the city
has been built since then,

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.637
is that they have quietly transformed

00:02:18.637 --> 00:02:22.130
the entire city into a built environment,

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:25.890
oriented around owning
and operating automobiles.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.450
Parking requirements for new buildings

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.150
were intended to prevent new residents

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:30.780
from competing for curb space.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.150
What the planners didn't anticipate

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:34.000
is that they would make cars and parking

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.820
the defining characteristic
of the urban landscape.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:40.340
Someone was going to build
a building on my street

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:41.800
and I was worried that

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.110
it was going to screw up
the parking on the curb

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:45.850
where I put my car.

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:48.540
And so we passed a law

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:49.850
and because of that law,

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:51.810
I got the building I wanted,

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.040
it had a parking lot next to it.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.705
And then we kept doing that for 70 years

00:02:57.705 --> 00:03:00.235
and everybody got the building they want,

00:03:00.235 --> 00:03:03.235
and nobody got the city they wanted.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:09.520
Parking lots and garages

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.190
proliferated in American cities.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:12.610
And especially in Los Angeles

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:14.060
where the number of parking spaces

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.040
grew decade after decade,

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:18.640
the requirements were such
a big part of city life

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.770
that allowing developers
to build less parking

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:22.490
than required by the zoning code

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:23.520
became a bargaining chip

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.520
between city planners and developers.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:27.430
And so the way this might work would be

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.070
developer has a plan

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:30.980
for an apartment building with 50 units

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:31.900
or something like that

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:34.050
and either through the State Government

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:36.100
or the local government strikes a deal

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:37.970
where the developer can take some

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:40.070
combination of extra density,

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:41.340
they can build more units,

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.630
reduce parking, some more height,

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:45.110
if for instance,

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:47.650
they set aside 15% of their units

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:50.190
as affordable housing
for lower income people.

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:52.140
This concern is misplaced.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.970
We know that parking requirements

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:56.346
do a lot of damage.

00:03:56.346 --> 00:03:57.560
But the way they encourage driving,

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.340
the way they reduce density,

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:02.610
reduce walk-ability, debase
the built environment,

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.860
undermine walking and so forth,

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.390
we shouldn't just keep them on the books

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:08.630
because occasionally we get some

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:10.700
affordable housing out of it.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.410
Reducing unnecessary parking requirements

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:14.510
would allow all developers
to build more housing,

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:15.610
creating more supply

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:17.770
and reducing the cost
of housing for everyone.

00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:19.600
When developers need a special arrangement

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.290
or variants to diverge from
the official zoning code,

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.140
rules aren't consistently applied.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:26.240
Same goes for restaurant
commercial spaces.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.430
Most restaurants in LA

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:31.890
don't have onsite parking
that satisfies zoning code.

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:34.470
The Taco Bells, the
McDonald's, the Sizzlers,

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.520
those are the ones that
satisfy parking code.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:37.800
Mott Smith, whose company rents out

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:38.760
commercial kitchen space,

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:39.593
is also a member of

00:04:39.593 --> 00:04:41.210
the Council of Infill Builders

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:42.170
and advocates for easing

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:43.900
parking requirements for restaurants.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:47.200
Everybody else is either going
into a grandfathered space

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:48.980
or they're getting a variance.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:52.720
And if you want to know
why food is expensive

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.410
and why it's hard for
restaurants to survive,

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.230
this is reason number one,

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:58.660
is that we make them comply

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:01.667
with these way outdated
parking requirements.

